"general_background":[{"id":"ode-solvent","title":"Octadecene is not always inert","summary":"Octadecene provides a weakly coordinating medium for hot solution growth, leaving the ligands more freedom to control surface binding. It can also polymerize under growth conditions, leaving an alkene-derived residue that is difficult to separate from hydrophobic nanocrystals.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/acs.nanolett.9b03088","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.nanolett.9b03088","title":"The Trouble with ODE: Polymerization during Nanocrystal Synthesis","authors":["Evert Dhaene","Jonas Billet","Ellie Bennett","Isabel Van Driessche","Jonathan De Roo"],"year":2019,"metadata_response_sha256":"76d6a9889aa3c4081d11d7cd082d3a30ac0153629988595d1deefc8393a00158","resolver_redirect_verified":true,"exact_DOI_metadata_verified":true,"paper_or_abstract_requested":false},"reagent_specific":true,"topic":"ode-polymerization"},{"id":"hot-injection-nucleation","title":"Nucleation and size focusing","summary":"A rapid change in precursor availability can create a short nucleation period followed by growth on existing particles. Growth can narrow the size distribution while material is supplied, whereas later ripening can consume smaller particles and change that distribution again.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr400544s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr400544s","title":"Mechanisms of Nucleation and Growth of Nanoparticles in Solution","authors":["Nguyen T. K. Thanh","N. Maclean","S. Mahiddine"],"year":2014,"metadata_response_sha256":"997f5bb631896f547843d7cf01573d11c3d62d6de6a941962d5c52a408320998","resolver_redirect_verified":true,"exact_DOI_metadata_verified":true,"paper_or_abstract_requested":false},"reagent_specific":false,"topic":"nucleation-and-focusing"}]}
